From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 17:13:16 -0500
Subject: fix: survive a tab's dialog that closes as it shows

On the Mac the browser died as a tab came back: TabDidEnterForeground showed the
tab's dialog, and NativeWidgetMac::Show went on through its WeakPtr to the widget,
which had gone while AppKit brought the window forward. Past that check stood
another: Widget::Show checks that the widget outlived the native show, which is
the same crash on Windows, and TabDialogManager::UpdateDialogVisibility then read
widget_, which is null by then.

A widget may go during a native show: NativeWidgetAura::Show says so, and
Widget::Hide already allows for it. NativeWidgetMac::Show now ends there as the
Aura one does, Widget::Show and ShowInactive end there as Hide does, and the
dialog manager reads no widget it no longer has.

A peek over the page goes with its widget at once, so the same close took the
peek and its page from under PeekOverlay::Show and OpenPeek. Both now stop.

The trigger in the report is not known. It is not an observer of the widget's
visibility, since ~Widget refuses that with a check of its own, so it ran from
something else AppKit does as the window comes forward.

Why: a dialog closing at the wrong moment must not kill the browser (BACKLOG
S-429, GitHub issue 82, tabs R67).

Removable when: upstream's NativeWidgetMac::Show and Widget::Show allow for a
widget that goes during the show.
